    "body": "Hi and thanks for the feedback.\r\n\r\nPySCF is supported but not fully integrated. Currently you need to feed the one- and two-body integrals manually to the initialization. But then you have access to a range of it's methods. This is for example used in combination with directly determined orbitals (through madness) in order to compute standard energies (ccsd, ccsd(t), fci) with them. \r\n\r\nif you have the molecular integrals (two body terms in openfermion ordering) ready, you can use it like this\r\n`mol = tq.Molecule(geometry=\"be 0.0 0.0 0.0\", backend=\"pyscf\", one_body_integrals=h, two_body_integrals=g, nuclear_repulsion=1.0, basis_set=\"dummy\")`\r\nand then for example\r\n`mol.compute_energy(\"ccsd\")`\r\n\r\nif you have the two-body integrals as numpy array, you can switch between the orderings via\r\ng = tq.chemistry.qc_base.NBodyTensor(elems=array, ordering='name')\r\ng.reorder(to='name')\r\nwith name = [\"openfermion\", \"mulliken\", \"dirac\"] \r\n\r\nIn the madness backend this is automatized for the classical methods. See for example here\r\nhttps://github.com/aspuru-guzik-group/tequila-tutorials/blob/main/ChemistrySeparablePairAnsatz.ipynb\r\n\r\nYou are probably looking for something like\r\n`mol = tq.Molecule(geometry=\"be 0.0 0.0 0.0\", basis_set=\"sto-3g\")`\r\nthat computes the integrals automatically through pyscf?\r\nCurrently that is not fully automatized, but I can look into it, if you need it.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
